# Future Directions

## Result

For a finite simple graph with $m$ edges and no isolated vertices among its $n$ vertices, the elementary consequences of the ratio $\binom{n}{2}/m$ form a complete chain:

1. the real closed form $2\binom{n}{2}=n(n-1)$;
2. positivity of $\binom{n}{2}$ for $n\geq2$;
3. the universal lower bound $1\leq\binom{n}{2}/m$ for a nonempty simple graph;
4. the handshake bound $n\leq2m$ when every vertex is non-isolated;
5. the resulting upper bound $\binom{n}{2}/m\leq n-1$;
6. membership of the threshold in $[1,n-1]$ for a nonempty graph without isolated vertices;
7. the contrarian conclusion that every probability $p<1$ automatically satisfies $p<\binom{n}{2}/m$;
8. the equivalent strict edge-budget inequality $pm<\binom{n}{2}$.

## Interpretation

The bare condition $p<\binom{n}{2}/m$ is weak when $p$ is a genuine probability below one: because a simple graph has at most $\binom{n}{2}$ edges, the ratio is always at least one. Thus this inequality alone cannot distinguish sparse from dense simple graphs in the usual probability range. Any stronger future claim needs additional structure beyond this threshold inequality.

## Next steps

A meaningful continuation would add the omitted conclusion of the truncated original claim and test whether it follows from the threshold or requires hypotheses such as connectedness, minimum degree, regularity, or a specified random-graph model. Sharpness examples can then be developed further: complete graphs attain the lower endpoint $1$, while matchings on all vertices attain the upper endpoint $n-1$ when $n$ is even.

